  "narrative": "I registered follow-up comparisons that address three complementary questions: whether the large accepted dataset associations persist in important co-alteration and technical contexts, whether lineage or driver context modifies the accepted dependencies, and whether the borderline KRAS and EGFR effects become clinically meaningful in biologically defined subsets. For KEAP1 loss and NFE2L2 dependency, the accepted direction is weaker NFE2L2 dependency, registered as positive signed effects; therefore the new conditional claims test whether this association persists among TP53-intact, KRAS-mutant, and EGFR-amplified cells. If the signed effects remain large and positive, the KEAP1/NFE2L2 finding is robust across driver contexts; if absent or much smaller in a context, the overall association may be co-alteration driven or context-specific. For TP53 loss and USP7 dependency, the validated accepted direction is weaker USP7 dependency, so I tested the association among KEAP1-loss cells and in batch_a. These audits address co-alteration and technical heterogeneity. For SMARCA4 loss and stronger SMARCA2 dependency, I proposed adenocarcinoma, ARID1A-loss, and non-Avana library conditional means. The adenocarcinoma context is biologically and numerically informative; ARID1A-loss context tests whether the SMARCA4 effect remains large when a related SWI/SNF component is also lost, complementing the ARID1A-intact specificity result; the non-Avana context checks whether the large association is an Avana-library artifact. For WRN and MSI-high, the accepted direction is stronger WRN dependency; I tested the association among higher mutation-burden samples to assess whether MSI-high status and mutation burden convey overlapping biological information. For KRAS and EGFR dependency, the overall effects have been unresolved and below or near the 10% relative threshold. I therefore registered driver-context tests: KRAS-mutation effect among TP53-intact cells and EGFR-amplification effect among KRAS-wildtype cells. These contexts are common enough for the comparison-cell rule and could reveal a subgroup where the association crosses the clinical-significance threshold. If these conditional estimates remain small or interval-crossing, the simplest conclusion is that these predictors do not have scientifically meaningful differences in dependency within this dataset. These are associations in the supplied data, not causal or externally generalizable claims.",
