  "narrative": "The newly delivered independent validation strongly confirms the two major accepted associations and a key subgroup validation. All estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For H12, TP53 loss/USP7 dependency, the validation signed estimate is +0.797 with an independent interval of approximately +0.769 to +0.824. Against the absolute overall USP7 mean of about 0.093, the 10% practical threshold is about 0.0093, so the validated association is overwhelmingly above that threshold and entirely positive. This supports weaker USP7 dependency in TP53-loss cells and confirms the discovery effect; I accept and close H12. For H7, SMARCA4 loss/SMARCA2 dependency, the validation signed estimate is +0.807 with interval approximately +0.777 to +0.838. The practical threshold for SMARCA2 is about 0.0415; the interval is far above that level. Combined with the precise discovery estimate and the supportive conditional analyses in batch_a, adenocarcinoma, ARID1A-loss, and non-Avana contexts, this validates that SMARCA4-loss cells have much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, i.e. a much more negative SMARCA2 score. I accept and close H7. The accepted H35 batch_a interaction remains meaningful as a context effect: the SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2 dependency effect is attenuated in batch_a relative to its complement, but the main effect is so large and now independently validated that further pursuit of this technical interaction is not the most informative next step. I close H35. For H24, MSI-high/WRN dependency within adenocarcinoma, the validation signed estimate is +0.784 with interval approximately +0.727 to +0.840. The WRN practical threshold is about 0.0301, so this subgroup effect is also overwhelmingly above the clinical threshold and fully supported. This validates the adenocarcinoma-specific MSI-high/WRN association and reinforces H13. I accept and close H24. H13 remains accepted as an overall in-dataset association because the discovery overall effect is very large and precise, and it is further supported by the adenocarcinoma validation and by the higher-mutation-burden subset effect. I keep H13 active rather than closing it only because independent validation of the overall MSI-high/WRN effect would be a valuable way to distinguish robust in-dataset association from subgroup or discovery-set stability; if validation capacity permits, it is a high-value next request. The validated accepted findings are therefore SMARCA4 loss/stronger SMARCA2 dependency, KEAP1 loss/weaker NFE2L2 dependency, TP53 loss/weaker USP7 dependency, and MSI-high/stronger WRN dependency in adenocarcinoma, with overall MSI-high/WRN supported but not yet overall-independent validated. These are dataset associations, not causal statements or claims of external generalizability. Negative or weak evidence remains for BRFAF V600E/BRAF dependency, ERBB2 amplification/ERBB2 dependency, MYC amplification/POLR2A dependency, ARID1A loss/SMARCA2 dependency without SMARCA4 loss, STK11 loss/NFE2L2 dependency, and RB1 loss/KIF18A dependency. The KRAS mutation/KRAS dependency and EGFR amplification/EGFR dependency effects remain scientifically borderline: multiple overall, lineage, and driver-context checks have point estimates near but generally below the 10% threshold or with intervals crossing zero and threshold, so I do not accept them as clinically meaningful associations in this supplied dataset. Next, I registered focused follow-ups: EGFR amplification among TP53-intact cells and KRAS mutation among EGFR-wildtype cells to test whether borderline oncogene effects become meaningful in mutually relevant driver backgrounds; MSI-high/WRN among lower mutation-burden samples to test independence from high mutation burden; KEAP1 loss/NFE2L2 among EGFR-wildtype cells as another co-alteration robustness check; and EGFR amplification/STAT3 dependency as a pathway-level cross-talk hypothesis. These comparisons remain in-dataset exploratory tests of association and specificity.",
