pplied dataset: supported and independently validated associations are SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, and CDKN2A loss with much stronger MDM2 dependency specifically in TP53-intact cells. These are robust across many within-dataset subgroups and library or lineage checks. Negative or unsupported findings include BRAF V600E and RIT1, KRAS mutation among TP53-intact cells, NF1 loss among KRAS wild-type cells, MYC-amplification associations for POLR2A and KIF18A, broad EGFR, ERBB2, PIK3CA, STK11, PTEN, and RIT1 bypass tests, and MTAP loss as an MDM2 modifier. Residual uncertainty concerns whether the CDKN2A-MDM2 effect is confined to TP53-intact cells, whether any TP53-loss effect is real but underpowered, and whether technical batch or 9p21 copy-number strata modulate it. The most informative next analyses are H117, which tests CDKN2A loss and stronger MDM2 dependency without TP53 eligibility, and H118, which tests CDKN2A loss among TP53-loss and MTAP-intact cells. H117 will distinguish a broad CDKN2A-MDM2 association from the validated TP53-intact conditional association; H118 will determine whether any apparent TP53-loss effect persists independently of MTAP co-deletion. All claims remain associations in the supplied dataset and should not be interpreted as causal or externally generalizable without additional validation.",
