th stronger MDM2 dependency in multiple contexts. The CDKN2A-MDM2 association is broad, H117 +0.1169 with interval 0.0823 to 0.1516, and is accepted in several accepted localizations, including TP53-intact cells H96 +0.1185 discovery and +0.1222 validated with adjusted lower bound 0.0642, MTAP-intact cells H119/H118, MTAP-loss cells H126, and low-copy 9p21 subsets H102/H127. The validated acceptance of H118 closes the specific concern that the CDKN2A-MDM2 effect disappears when MTAP loss is excluded in the TP53-loss population.\n\nNegative or unsupported findings. Several initially plausible predictor-outcome pairs are not supported. BRAF V600E is not associated with BRAF dependency, H2 and H77 rejected. ERBB2 amplification is not associated with ERBB2 dependency, H4 rejected. KRAS mutation is not supported as a broad predictor of KRAS dependency: H1 is near null, H19 is near null in adenocarcinoma, and the TP53-intact localization H49 was validated near null. MYC amplification does not support dependency changes for POLR2A, RPL11, DHX9, or accepted MYC-expression subsets, H9/H10/H11/H132/H133. PTEN loss is not associated with EGFR or KRAS dependency, H88/H89. RB1 loss is not associated with KIF18A dependency, H14. PIK3CA mutation is not associated with KRAS dependency in KRAS wild-type cells, H100. NF1 loss is not supported as an association with KRAS dependency in KRAS wild-type cells after independent validation of H67 was null and opposite. EGFR amplification is not a supported broad predictor of EGFR dependency: H3 was borderline and H51 was validated opposite; related EGFR subgroup results H20/H50 remain near null and should not be interpreted as supported. TP53 loss is not accepted as an MDM2 predictor, H6/H21 are unresolved/near null and should not be treated as supported. MTAP loss is not accepted as an independent modifier after CDKN2A-loss context in the main validated tests; H103 was rejected because validation was opposite, and H106/H107 rejected direct MTAP tests in several TP53-intact CDKN2A contexts. Several MTAP or 9p21 localization tests remain imprecise, H110/H111/H114/H120/H121/H136/H137/H138/H139.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. The main remaining uncertainty is attribution within the CDKN2A/MTAP/9p21 deletion-like genomic context. The broad TP53-loss CDKN2A-MDM2 effect H108 remains unresolved: discovery +0.1106, interval 0.0350 to 0.1862, but validation +0.1010, adjusted interval -0.0003 to 0.2023. The direction is consistent but the adjusted interval does not exclude null or clear the practical threshold, so it is not accepted and cannot be reconciled by H134 because those TP53-loss MTAP-loss cells were too sparse. KRAS mutation and EGFR amplification associations remain unresolved only as borderline nulls; they should not be treated as supported. NF1-loss KRAS contexts H68/H69 are imprecise and, because the broader NF1-KRAS test H67 was not validated, they remain unsupported. MTAP-independent effects are unresolved because direct MTAP tests are imprecise or null in several contexts, and the active follow-ups have not yet produced evidence. Low-copy 9p21 interactions H112/H138 are not supported, so the accepted CDKN2A-MDM2 association should not be mechanistically attributed specifically to MTAP loss or 9p21 copy number from current evidence.\n\nMost informative next analysis. No new proposals are added because the two most informative next comparisons are already active: H142, MTAP loss among all CDKN2A-intact cells, and H143, MTAP loss among low-copy 9p21 CDKN2A-intact cells for MDM2 dependency. H142 asks whether MTAP loss has any MDM2 association independent of CDKN2A loss. H143 asks whether low-copy 9p21 context makes an MTAP-independent signal more apparent. A positive H142 or H143 interval excluding null and exceeding the MDM2 threshold would support an MTAP-associated signal distinct from accepted CDKN2A-loss association; a near-null or imprecise result would favor interpreting the accepted CDKN2A-MDM2