  "narrative": "No validation response is due and independent validation capacity is exhausted, so there is no new validation to assess and no new validation request can be made. I reassessed a selected set of unresolved near-null associations and changed them to reject and closed because their signed 95% intervals are well below the approximate 0.10 dependency-score practical threshold used throughout this run, even where some intervals excluded zero. These are decisions about scientific and practical meaningfulness in the supplied dataset, not about whether tiny statistical contrasts exist.\n\nSupported dataset-specific associations. Direction convention: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has stronger, more negative dependency; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has weaker, less negative dependency. The strongest supported associations are very large and, for four major axes, independently validated. First, KEAP1 loss is associated with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, H18/R5/R26, with discovery and validation estimates about +0.80, and this holds across many internal contexts including adenocarcinoma H56/R41 and high MYC expression H243/R244. Second, TP53 loss is associated with much weaker USP7 dependency, H12/R10/R13, with discovery and validation estimates about +0.80, and with support across several contexts including adenocarcinoma H25/R22, CDKN2A-loss H131/R126, and research_signature_c contexts H166/R170. Third, SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, H7/R7/R39, with discovery and validation estimates about +0.78 to +0.81, and robustness across ARID1A states, batch, non-Avana library, high MYC expression, high EMT, high stemness, and other strata such as H244/R245 and H247/R248. Fourth, MSI-high cells show much stronger WRN dependency, H13/R11/R53, with discovery and validation estimates about +0.77 to +0.79, and robustness in adenocarcinoma H24/R23/R40 and SLFN11-high cells H240/R241. Fifth, CDKN2A loss is associated with stronger MDM2 dependency, H117/R108, with the strongest and most repeated internal support in TP53-intact cells, H96/R70/R93, around +0.12 in discovery and validation, and replicated in several strata including MTAP-intact cells H113/R102, adenocarcinoma H98/R83, low copy_number_9p21 H127/R122, high MYC expression H228/R230, and PTEN-loss cells H207/R209. Sixth, PTEN loss is associated with much stronger STAT3 dependency, H184/R188, estimate about +0.27, and this association is internally robust across many contexts, including TP53-intact H187/R191, TP53-loss H188/R192, KRAS-wild-type H206/R208, KRAS-mutant H225/R227, PIK3CA-mutant H214/R216, PIK3CA-intact H219/R221, high MYC expression H226/R228, adenocarcinoma H229/R231, high IFN-gamma H241/R242, high hypoxia H242/R243, and high EMT H246/R247. PTEN-STAT3 is accepted as a supplied-dataset association, but unlike the four very large axes it lacks independent validation, so its external or mechanistic interpretation remains more uncertain.\n\nNegative and unsupported findings. Direct target alterations generally do not predict their own dependency scores in this dataset. BRAF V600E is not meaningfully associated with BRAF dependency, H2/R2 and H77/R60. KRAS mutation is not meaningfully associated with KRAS dependency, H1/R1 and validated near-null H49/R80. EGFR amplification is not meaningfully associated with EGFR dependency, H3/R3, and related validations or context restrictions do not rescue it, including H51/R133. ERBB2 amplification is not meaningfully associated with ERBB2 dependency, H4/R4, H78/R61, and H17/R144. MYC amplification is not supported for clinically meaningful dependency in POLR2A H9/R9, RPL11 H10/R116 and expression-restricted H133/R128, DHX9 H11/R142, KIF18A H94/R77 and H132/R127, USP7 H81/R132, or WRN; the opposite-direction WRN signal H189/R186 and high-expression refinement H191/R193 are far below practical threshold. RB1 loss is not meaningfully associated with KIF18A H14/R12, SMARCA2 H185/R189, or POLR2A H169/R173, H176/R180, H177/R181, and H235/R236. The constructed research markers D, E, and F do not show reliable predictive value in the targeted tests, H144/R148, H145/R149, H146/R150, H147/R151, and H148/R152, and interaction tests with these markers remain near null. Many smaller associations that were statistically detectable but far below practical importance are now rejected: NF1-loss KRAS-dependency signals in KRAS-wild-type contexts H68/R54, H69/R55, and adenocarcinoma H70/R56 are too small and inconsistent with broader attenuation H67/R66; ERBB2 amplification with STAT3 dependency in PTEN- or EGFR-intact cells H224/R226 and H227/R229 is far below threshold; STK11 loss with USP7 dependency H190/R190 is below threshold; and near-null expression-restricted MYC and RB1 comparisons are unsupported. These results argue against overinterpreting small, context-specific or nominally significant contrasts.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty remains mostly where effects are near threshold, imprecise, or dependent on small cell counts. CDKN2A loss with MDM2 dependency in TP53-loss cells is borderline and not firm, H108/R97/R106. CDKN2A-loss effects in high copy_number_9p21 contexts H111/R100 and H120/R111, in batch-restricted TP53-intact cells H110/R99, and in MTAP interaction models H114/R103 and H121/R112 remain unresolved because of small exposed cells, wide intervals, or inconsistent directions. NF1 loss may have a very small residual relationship to KRAS dependency, but it is not clinically meaningful in this dataset; the KRAS-mutant interaction H71/R57 is wide and should not be treated as evidence of biology. Some constructed research signature interactions, such as H151/R155, remain imprecise. RB1-POLR2A and several MYC-related refinements are closed as unsupported near-null. Interactions modifying PTEN-STAT3, including TP53, KRAS, or MYC-expression interactions H201/R203, H213/R215, and H220/R222, are not sufficiently precise to modify the broad PTEN-STAT3 conclusion.\n\nMost informative next analysis. Because independent validation is unavailable, the next most useful work is not more small subgroup mining but confounding and assay-robustness assessment of the few large or accepted unvalidated associations, especially PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency. Priority internal analyses would test screen quality and assay metadata: screen_roc_auc, screen_nnmd, cas9 activity, CRISPR library, batch, growth rate, culture type, ploidy, and aneuploidy, plus continuous IFN-gamma, hypoxia, and EMT interactions. Equivalent confirmation for the four very large validated axes using non-Avana library and high-quality-screen restrictions would help determine whether their magnitude is assay-dependent, although they already appear robust. These analyses remain internal and cannot substitute for independent validation. All conclusions are association-level in the supplied lung-lineage dataset. They do not establish causality, therapeutic relevance, or generalizability beyond this dataset; genetic co-occurrence, copy-number effects, culture or batch structure, screen quality, transcriptional state, and multiple testing remain plausible explanations for smaller effects.",
